\text{In Homotopy Analysis Method (HAM) the solution of a nonlinear problem is written as an infinite series}
\[
u(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} u_n(x),
\]
\text{where each term }u_n(x)\text{ is generated by continuously deforming a simple “starting” problem into the original one through an embedding (or homotopy) parameter }p\in[0,1]. 
\]
\text{The series is constructed so that}
\begin{itemize}
\item \text{the }n\text{-th term }u_n(x)\text{ contains the }n\text{-th order contribution of the deformation,}
\item \text{the series converges (for }p\to1\text{) to the exact solution of the original nonlinear equation,}
\item \text{the coefficients }u_n(x)\text{ are determined solely by the original differential equation and the chosen auxiliary linear operator,}
\item \text{as a power series in }p\text{ it can be written as }u(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}p^{\,n}u_n(x),\text{ and setting }p=1\text{ yields the desired function.}
\end{itemize}
\text{Thus the series provides a systematic analytic representation of the solution function, much like a Taylor expansion, but with the added flexibility of choosing the deformation path.  When the series converges, its sum is exactly the function that HAM seeks to approximate; consequently the series itself is the mathematical object that embodies the solution used in the Homotopy Analysis Method.}

